## Finding (Section C — Untrusted Input at Protocol Boundaries; hygiene)

In `crate-jmap-metadata-server/src/metadata.rs:471-479`:

```rust
for id_val in &destroy_arr {
    match id_val.as_str() {
        Some(s) => id_strs.push(s.to_owned()),
        None => {
            return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
                "destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {id_val}"
            )));
        }
    }
}
```

The error description interpolates the entire `id_val` via `{id_val}` Display. `serde_json::Value::Display` re-serializes the JSON value as text — so a destroy array element of `{"huge": "...10MB...", "nested": {"deeply": {...}}}` would produce an error message containing the full re-serialised JSON.

## What goes wrong if this stays as-is?

1. **Response-size amplification within HTTP body cap.** Bounded by the consumer's body-size limit (caller responsibility per `parse.rs:28-53`), but defense-in-depth fix is trivial: format only the JSON type tag, not the value.

2. **Echoing arbitrary client-controlled JSON in the `description` field.** RFC 8620 §3.6.1 specifies `description` as "an unstructured English text suitable for displaying to a developer". Echoing a megabyte-sized JSON blob there is unhelpful and creates an indirect oracle: a multi-tenant deployment that buffers the response body for logging would log the attacker-controlled JSON verbatim. Format-string injection is not possible here (Rust's `format!` is type-safe), but if downstream consumers parse the description for diagnostics, a malicious value could include strings shaped like other error markers.

3. **Distinct from finding #1 (Id boundary validation).** Even after Id::new_validated is wired into the destroy loop (the proper fix from JMAP-ayoz.39), the `id_val` here is being rejected because it is NOT a JSON string — it is some other JSON shape (null, object, array, number, bool). Validation of Id syntax happens AFTER unwrapping to `as_str()`. So this finding is independent of JMAP-ayoz.39.

## Ideal fix

Report only the JSON type tag of the offending value:

```rust
let type_name = match id_val {
    Value::Null => "null",
    Value::Bool(_) => "boolean",
    Value::Number(_) => "number",
    Value::Array(_) => "array",
    Value::Object(_) => "object",
    Value::String(_) => unreachable!(),
};
return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
    "destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {type_name}"
)));
```

This keeps the error description bounded to a small constant size and conveys the actionable diagnostic (wrong JSON type) without echoing attacker-controlled bytes.

## Scope note

The existing test `set_destroy_null_element_returns_invalid_arguments` (metadata.rs:838-848) only verifies the error type is `invalidArguments`; it does not assert anything about the description content, so the fix is wire-stable.

This is a hygiene finding (P3). Not workspace cookie-cut — the surrounding validate-and-collect pass was added recently (bbde4c3) and is metadata-server-specific in shape. The same fix may be valuable in sibling extension-servers but is not mechanical-copy from a canonical.
